Q: I already know Spring Boot — what should I prepare for Quarkus interviews?

Goal: map your Spring knowledge → Quarkus equivalents, plus Quarkus-only strengths (startup, memory, native).

────────────────────────────────────────
P0 — Must speak confidently (tonight/tomorrow)

1) What Quarkus is + why it exists (cloud-native, fast start, low RSS, native images)
2) Build-time augmentation vs Spring runtime auto-config/reflection
3) Annotation mapping cheat sheet (Inject/Path/Transactional/Panache)
4) Similarities to Spring Boot (properties, REST, JPA, Kafka, Docker/K8s)
5) Differences (CDI/JAX-RS/Panache/Mutiny/extensions, no @SpringBootApplication bootstrap)
6) One end-to-end REST + DB example in Quarkus style
7) When choose Quarkus vs when stay on Spring Boot

Files in this folder:
- What is Quarkus
- Quarkus vs Spring Boot
- Spring Boot to Quarkus cheat sheet
- Quarkus REST API implementation
- Quarkus CDI and beans
- Quarkus Hibernate ORM with Panache
- Quarkus Dev Mode Native and GraalVM
- Quarkus Security JWT and OIDC (overview)

────────────────────────────────────────
P1 — Strong follow-ups

- Quarkus extensions ≈ Spring starters
- application.properties / profiles (%dev, %prod)
- Health/metrics: /q/health, Micrometer
- RestClient (@RegisterRestClient)
- Reactive: Uni/Multi (Mutiny) vs Mono/Flux
- Transactions: jakarta.transaction.@Transactional
- Testing: @QuarkusTest
- Kafka with SmallRye Reactive Messaging (concept)

────────────────────────────────────────
P2 — Nice extras

- Native image limitations (reflection config, closed world)
- Quarkus Spring Compatibility extensions (spring-web/di/data-jpa) — migration bridge, not long-term idiomatic
- Kubernetes-friendly: small image, fast scale-to-zero

────────────────────────────────────────
Honesty strategy (you are Spring-first)

“I’ve built production systems with Spring Boot (APIs, JPA, Security, Kafka).
For Quarkus I’ve prepared the programming model mapping — CDI, JAX-RS, Panache —
and the architectural reasons teams pick Quarkus: build-time processing, faster startup, lower memory, native compilation.
I can ramp to idiomatic Quarkus quickly because concepts map 1:1 from Spring.”

Do NOT pretend deep production Quarkus unless true.
Do show you can translate Spring patterns correctly.

────────────────────────────────────────
60-minute drill plan

0–10  What is Quarkus + why (native/containers)
10–25 Cheat sheet annotations aloud
25–40 Speak REST+Panache example end-to-end
40–50 Quarkus vs Spring compare table
50–60 Q&A: self-invocation? transactions? Kafka? security?

────────────────────────────────────────
Quick win sentence

“Quarkus feels familiar if you know Spring Boot — same REST+DI+JPA ideas —
but it moves configuration and wiring to build time so apps start faster and use less memory, especially in containers and native mode.”
